---
description: Rules for interactive prototypes
alwaysApply: true
---

# Interaction design

When building prototypes, implement the interaction rather than only reproducing static screens.

Buttons should work.

Navigation should work.

Dialogs should open and close.

Menus should open.

Forms should accept input.

Validation should be demonstrable.

Loading should be demonstrable.

Error scenarios should be demonstrable.

# Fake backend

Use deterministic mock behavior where possible.

Example:

valid@example.com
→ success

error@example.com
→ server error

taken@example.com
→ already exists

This allows a designer or researcher to reproduce scenarios during testing.

# Timing

Short artificial loading states may be used to simulate real product behavior.

Prefer approximately:

300–800 ms

unless the prompt specifies otherwise.

# Navigation

Keep navigation simple.

Prefer:
- React Router;
- local state for small flows;
- URL parameters when they make prototype scenarios easier to share.

# Prototype controls

When useful, expose prototype-only controls for:

- scenario;
- user role;
- account state;
- feature flag;
- loading/error state.

Prototype controls must not visually interfere with the actual product UI.
